- op: create  # 0ef5
  at: '2026-07-31T07:53:34Z'  # 0ef5
  by: agent  # 0ef5
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0ef5
  clock: 1  # 0ef5
  nonce: 0ef5  # 0ef5
  data:  # 0ef5
    kind: work_item  # 0ef5
    title: "~30 dead 'span is None' guards defend an impossible state while the reachable one (Span(0,0,0,0) from a missing span key) passes through"  # 0ef5
    status: todo_hard  # 0ef5
    priority: 2  # 0ef5
    parent:  # 0ef5
    tags:  # 0ef5
    - mypy_adoption  # 0ef5
    - infra  # 0ef5
    isbefore: []  # 0ef5
    duplicate_of: []  # 0ef5
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 0ef5
    pr_ref:  # 0ef5
    description: "**Mechanic**: Roughly 30 sites across core and the language packages carry a defensive guard of the form `if sym.span is None: continue`, almost all annotated `# pragma: no cover - defensive for malformed symbols`. That condition can never be true, and the malformed state it is named for takes a different shape entirely, which passes the guard untouched.\n\n**Why it can never be true** (three independent paths, all checked):\n- `Symbol.span` is declared `span: Span` in `ir.py` — non-Optional AND a required positional field with no default, so a Symbol cannot be constructed without one.\n- Producer audit: `rg -o \"span=None\" --glob 'packages/*/src/**/*.py'` returns **zero** sites. No emitter passes None.\n- Deserialization, the one plausible route: `Symbol.from_dict` does `span_data = d.get(\"span\", {})` then `span=Span.from_dict(span_data)`. A cached artifact with no `span` key therefore yields a real Span object, never None.\n\n**The state that CAN occur, and which the guard does not catch**: `Span.from_dict({})` returns `Span(start_line=0, end_line=0, start_col=0, end_col=0)` — verified live. So a Symbol deserialized from an artifact missing its span carries a DEGENERATE span, not a null one. Every one of these guards lets it through, then downstream code does span arithmetic on zeros: a symbol that claims to occupy line 0 to line 0. Anything doing containment by span nesting (the containment linker's Phase 2, the G2 emission-parity columns added this week) will treat it as enclosing nothing and being enclosed by nothing.\n\n**This is the label-before-value shape (R7 / L30)**: a check was written against a plausible-sounding predicate (`is None`) rather than the one the data can actually violate (`is degenerate`). Fixing the number — deleting the dead guard — without ruling on the predicate would remove the marker that something here was meant to be defended, and leave the real hole open.\n\n**How it surfaced**: as ~30 `[unreachable]` errors in the mypy strict surface. Worth recording as method — the errors are not type-hygiene noise; each one is mypy reporting that a branch a human deliberately wrote can never execute, and a cluster of 30 sharing one predicate is a single design fact, not 30 chores.\n\n**Scope**: the `unreachable` code accounts for 30 of the 1080 strict errors. Not all 30 are this predicate — a sample showed most are (`linkers/registry.py:298`, `linkers/registry.py:1019`, and siblings), with at least one different shape (`analyze/base.py:2931` guards a re-assigned local, `lua.py:840` guards `tree.root_node`). The exact split needs the full list walked before any deletion.\n\n**Decision this item must make, not settled here**: three options, and the choice is a design call rather than a cleanup.\n1. **Delete the guards.** Correct per the type contract, removes ~30 dead branches and ~30 `# pragma: no cover` markers (a coverage-honesty gain — those pragmas currently assert \"untestable\" about code that is in fact unreachable). Leaves the degenerate-span hole undefended.\n2. **Re-point the guards** at the state that can occur — a zero/degenerate span predicate, probably shared (`Span.is_degenerate()`), so the check is defined once rather than re-derived at 30 sites. Keeps the defensive intent and makes it real. Needs a ruling on what a consumer should DO with a degenerate span, which is the actual open question.\n3. **Make the impossible state impossible to represent** and stop deserializing a missing span into `Span(0,0,0,0)` at all — either reject the record or carry the absence honestly. Most in keeping with the correctness strategy's chokepoint posture, largest blast radius.\n\n**Related**: WI-duguk (the G2 emission-parity columns measure span NESTING, so a degenerate span is exactly the input that would make a container silently root nothing); the `[unreachable]` cluster in INV-zogud's surface; ADR-0033 (a spec-vs-data validator is where a degenerate-span check would naturally live if option 2 or 3 is chosen)."  # 0ef5
    fields: {}  # 0ef5
- op: discuss  # f8bf
  at: '2026-07-31T10:03:57Z'  # f8bf
  by: agent  # f8bf
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f8bf
  clock: 2  # f8bf
  nonce: f8bf  # f8bf
  message: "SCOPE CORRECTION: this item is ~2.7x larger than filed, and it has a second half I missed.\n\nFiled as \"~30 dead `span is None` guards\" from the `[unreachable]` cluster. Re-measured on dev at 832 total strict errors, `Symbol.span` accounts for **82**:\n\n  * 52 of the 66 `[truthy-bool]` errors — `Member \"span\" has type \"Span\" which does not implement __bool__ or __len__ so it could always be true in boolean context`. These are `if X.span:` truthiness tests, ALWAYS TRUE.\n  * ~30 `[unreachable]` — the `if X.span is None: continue  # pragma: no cover - defensive` guards this item was filed for. ALWAYS FALSE.\n\nOne field's declaration therefore generates 10% of the remaining strict surface, in two mirror-image forms: half the sites test whether the span is present and half test whether it is absent, and neither test can ever change the outcome.\n\nHOW THE 52 BREAK DOWN (each line read, not sampled):\n  41  `line=X.span.start_line if X.span else 0`  — a ternary whose else-branch is unreachable. 36 as a keyword argument, 5 as a plain assignment.\n   3  `elif sym.span and src_sym.span:` (lua_ffi.py:195, pyffi.py:341, ruby_ffi.py:213) — NOT a duplicated condition; these are two DIFFERENT symbols, both always truthy, so the conjunction is always true. Recording this because a normalising regex makes them look like a copy-paste bug and they are not.\n   ~8 compound conditions (`if sym.kind in (...) and sym.path and X.span:`, `if not sub.path or not X.span:`) needing individual reading rather than a pattern rewrite.\n\nTHE SPLIT THIS SUGGESTS, which changes this item's shape:\n\nThe 41-site ternary group is NOT the three-way design decision this item describes. Replacing `X.span.start_line if X.span else 0` with `X.span.start_line` deletes a provably dead branch and preserves behaviour exactly; there is no question about what to do with a degenerate span because the expression never consults one. That is a mechanical, evidence-backed simplification.\n\nThe `is None: continue` group IS the design decision, because deleting a guard changes the shape of the enclosing loop and removes the marker that someone once intended a defence. That is where the delete / re-point-at-degenerate / make-impossible choice actually bites.\n\nRecommend splitting accordingly rather than treating all 82 as one call.\n\nON WHETHER THE DEGENERATE-SPAN HAZARD JUSTIFIES OPTION 2, since this item raised it and did not answer it: `Span.from_dict({})` yields `Span(0,0,0,0)`, but reaching that requires a serialized node with no `span` key at all. Every Symbol carries a required span, so serialization always writes one — the degenerate case is only reachable from a hand-edited or corrupted artifact. That is a real but narrow exposure, and critically it is one an `is None` check would never have caught either. So the existing guards are not \"defending the wrong thing badly\"; they are defending nothing at all, and the degenerate-span question is independent of whether they stay or go. Option 2 remains worth doing on its own merits; it is not a reason to keep these guards."  # f8bf
- op: discuss  # 0883
  at: '2026-07-31T10:22:41Z'  # 0883
  by: agent  # 0883
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0883
  clock: 3  # 0883
  nonce: '0883'  # 0883
  message: "CORRECTION — I tried the \"mechanical\" half of this and it was WRONG. Reverted. The previous note's claim that these guards \"are defending nothing at all\" is withdrawn.\n\nWHAT I DID: rewrote the 43 `line=X.span.start_line if X.span else 0` ternaries to `X.span.start_line`, on the reasoning that `Symbol.span` is declared non-Optional, is a required positional field, has zero `span=None` producers in `packages/*/src/`, and that `from_dict` always constructs a Span. The whole-tree ratchet went 832 -> 789, clean, no code grew.\n\nWHAT CAUGHT IT: the test suite, not the type checker. Two tests failed, and their names are the finding:\n\n  test_caddy_module_dispatch_linker.py::test_marker_without_span_emits_line_zero\n  test_kafka_streams_dispatch_linker.py::test_class_without_span_emits_line_zero\n\nBoth construct `Symbol(..., span=None)` explicitly and assert the edge comes out with `line == 0`. Python does not enforce annotations at runtime, so a span-less Symbol is constructible regardless of what the declaration says. The `else 0` branch is reachable and these tests reach it.\n\nTHE PRECISE SITUATION, which is neither \"dead guards\" nor \"live guards\":\n\n  * 0  sites in packages/*/src/ construct span=None. Production genuinely cannot produce one — synthesized boundary symbols get `Span(0,0,0,0)` (ir.py builds a `zero_span` for exactly this), never None.\n  * 10 TEST FILES construct span=None deliberately, and assert on what the guards do with it.\n\nSo the guards are LIVE ONLY UNDER TEST. They are kept alive by tests of a state production cannot reach. That is a third category this item did not previously name, and it changes what the decision is about: it is not only \"should these guards stay\", it is \"are those ten test files testing anything real\".\n\nALSO WITHDRAWN: the previous note's split into \"41 mechanical sites + 30 needing a design call\". There is no mechanical half. Every one of the 43 ternaries is load-bearing for at least the two tests above, and possibly more (I stopped after the first failure rather than mapping which of the 10 test files pin which of the 43 sites — that mapping is prerequisite work for any decision here).\n\nWHAT THIS SAYS ABOUT THE MYPY SIGNAL, worth carrying beyond this item: `truthy-bool` reports \"could always be true\" based on the DECLARED type. When the declaration is stricter than reality, the checker's confidence is inherited from the lie, and draining the error deletes a real guard. This is the same shape as the earlier `invoke.py` finding, where mypy called a live `if resolved is None:` branch unreachable because an injectable `which` parameter was annotated as returning plain `str`. Twice now the annotation was the wrong thing and the code was right. A \"the checker says this is unreachable\" argument is only as good as the annotation it rests on.\n\nTHE OPTION THIS OPENS, and probably the real answer: make the declaration honest — `Symbol.span: Optional[Span]`. That would make all ~82 span-related errors disappear for the RIGHT reason (the guards become well-typed rather than redundant), keep every test passing, and stop the declaration from claiming a guarantee the constructor does not enforce. Cost, unmeasured: every `sym.span.start_line` that currently has no guard becomes a `union-attr` error, and there are many. Someone should measure that number before choosing — it may be larger than the 82 it saves, which would itself be a useful fact."  # 0883
- op: discuss  # 4390
  at: '2026-07-31T10:24:12Z'  # 4390
  by: agent  # 4390
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4390
  clock: 4  # 4390
  nonce: '4390'  # 4390
  message: "MEASURED: `Symbol.span: Optional[Span]` is a NET WIN of 22 errors, and the cost side is information rather than noise. This resolves the \"unmeasured\" caveat in my previous note, and I think it resolves the three-way design question this item was filed with.\n\nExperiment: changed the one declaration in ir.py, ran the whole tree, reverted. No commit.\n\n  code              now   Optional   delta\n  truthy-bool        66      14       -52\n  union-attr         30      72       +42\n  unreachable        30      23        -7\n  redundant-expr     20      15        -5\n  TOTAL             832     810       -22\n\nThe -52 matches exactly the 52 `Member \"span\" has type \"Span\"` sites counted independently, so the mechanism is confirmed rather than inferred: those checks stop being \"always true\" because the value genuinely can be None.\n\nWHY THE +42 IS THE POINT, NOT THE PRICE. Each new `union-attr` is a site that does `sym.span.<attr>` with no guard at all. Set against the 43 sites that DO guard, the picture is a codebase split roughly half and half on whether a span-less Symbol is possible — i.e. there is no consistent contract today, and the declaration currently sides with the half that would raise AttributeError. Those 42 sites are not new defects introduced by the annotation; they are pre-existing unguarded accesses that the declaration is hiding. Making the declaration honest is what surfaces them.\n\nTHE THREE OPTIONS THIS ITEM WAS FILED WITH ARE NOW ANSWERABLE:\n\n  1. Delete the guards — REFUTED by test. Two tests construct Symbol(span=None) and assert line 0; ten test files do it in total. Deleting is a live behaviour change, verified the hard way.\n  2. Re-point at a degenerate-span predicate — still available, still independent, and NOT what the guards are for. Note that Span(0,0,0,0) is truthy (no __bool__/__len__), so no truthiness check ever distinguished it.\n  3. Make the impossible state impossible — the inverse turns out to be right. The state is not impossible; the DECLARATION is wrong. `span: Optional[Span]` is the honest form, it costs -22 errors, and it converts every existing guard from redundant-per-the-checker into correct-per-the-checker.\n\nRECOMMENDED, with the residual work stated plainly: adopt `Optional[Span]`, then work the 42 union-attr sites individually. Each is a real decision — add a guard, or narrow with an assert where the caller genuinely established presence. That is ~42 sites of honest work replacing 82 errors of dishonest silence. It should NOT be done as a blanket sweep; the whole lesson of this item is that a uniform rewrite across span sites is exactly what breaks.\n\nWHAT I GOT WRONG, twice, recorded because the pattern is the useful part:\n  * First I claimed the guards were dead and rewrote 43 of them. The ratchet went 832 -> 789 CLEAN. The test suite caught it. mypy was correct given the declaration; the declaration was the lie.\n  * Then I claimed the population split into a \"mechanical 41\" and a \"design-call 30\". There is no mechanical half — every ternary is load-bearing for at least the two failing tests.\nThe general form, now three-for-three this session (invoke.py's `which` parameter, this, and the earlier annotation errors): AN ARGUMENT OF THE FORM \"THE CHECKER SAYS THIS IS UNREACHABLE\" IS ONLY AS GOOD AS THE ANNOTATION IT RESTS ON. When the annotation is stricter than reality, the checker inherits the lie and its confidence is worthless — and draining the error deletes real code."  # 4390
